Write a BCP 47 stream language into an ISO 639-2 field as the code of its language.

FFmpeg's "language" tag holds a BCP 47 tag whenever the source gave one: the HLS demuxer copies a
rendition's LANGUAGE, which RFC 8216 defines as an RFC 5646 tag, the DASH demuxer copies lang, and
the Matroska demuxer reads LanguageBCP47 once 0006 is applied. MKVToolNix writes an English track
there as "en" alone. Three writers whose field holds a three-letter ISO 639-2 code took such a tag
as if it were one. movenc packed its first three characters, refused "en" and "pt-BR" and wrote the
unspecified code, so the track read back with no language. mpegtsenc left an audio stream's
language descriptor out, and wrote "zh-Hant" as the two codes "zh-" and "ant" for DVB subtitles
and teletext. matroskaenc wrote the tag into Language, which holds a code, and never wrote
LanguageBCP47.

ff_lang_tag_to_iso639_2 finds the code of the language a tag's first subtag names, through the
table ff_convert_lang_to already uses, and says whether the tag is a BCP 47 tag or a code with
subtags such as Matroska's "fre-ca". MP4 writes the terminological code, as ISO/IEC 14496-12 asks,
MOV looks for either code in its Macintosh table, and MPEG-TS and Matroska write the bibliographic
code, as MKVToolNix does. Matroska also writes the whole tag as LanguageBCP47, which a reader
prefers, except in WebM, whose element list has no such element. A three-letter tag, a comma list
of them, and a code with subtags are written exactly as before.

ff_convert_lang_to now scans its table instead of bisecting it. The ISO 639-1 part ends with six
deprecated codes appended out of order after "zu", so the binary search never found "yo", "za",
"zh" or "zu": a Chinese track could not be mapped here, and the ASF and MXF demuxers, which map
a stream's 2-letter tag through it, gave such a stream no language at all.

Applied by BuildFFmpegTask before configure, after 0004. Fixes yuroyami/KiteFFmpeg#156.

